---
/**
 * About: who builds SAIPEN and why, how the work is done, the builder
 * principles behind it, and the engineering contract this site holds itself
 * to (MASTER_ROADMAP M14-M17, M25).
 *
 * Author facts are limited to what the author publishes on GitHub (profile
 * README, SAIPEN README and GUIDE); no biography, location or credentials are
 * invented. The dogfooding section is read from this repository's own
 * .saipen/ files at build time.
 */
import MaturityBadge from '../../components/MaturityBadge.astro';
import PixelMark from '../../components/PixelMark.astro';
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site';
import { ECOSYSTEM_SNAPSHOT, PROJECTS } from '../../data/ecosystem';
import { LAST_VERIFICATION } from '../../data/verification';
import { SHORT_COMMIT, meta } from '../../lib/canonical';
import { readSelfState } from '../../lib/selfstate';

const self = readSelfState();
const done = self?.tickets.filter((t) => t.section === 'DONE') ?? [];

const GATES = [
  ['npm run build', 'Static production build; content front-matter is schema-validated here.'],
  ['npm run check', 'Astro and TypeScript type check.'],
  ['npm run lint', 'Style law: no radius, shadow, blur, transparency, motion, hex colour, em unit or size without its pixel face.'],
  ['npm run validate:themes', 'All 16 palettes carry exactly the 21 tokens.'],
  ['npm run validate:canonical', 'The protocol snapshot still matches its recorded SHA-256 hashes.'],
  ['npm run validate:content', 'Every internal link and anchor in the built site resolves; no duplicate ids; required metadata present.'],
  ['npm run validate:baselines', 'Every versioned screenshot baseline is present and matches the manifest, so an archive cannot silently drop the visual evidence.'],
  ['npm run audit:build', 'Structure, theme contract, route registry, performance budget and protocol data of the built output.'],
  ['npm run test:pixel', 'Every pixel of every page is one of the palette colours, in Chromium and WebKit.'],
  ['npm run test:shell', 'Routes, maturity badges, navigation, breadcrumbs, landmarks, overflow at 320 / 390 / 640 / 1280 px.'],
  ['npm run test:content', 'Docs chrome, spec visualizer and aliases, playground, search, agent-readable files.'],
  ['npm run test:runtime', 'Theme persistence, pre-paint restore, fallback and nested palette isolation.'],
  ['npm run test:visual', 'Screenshot baselines, compared with zero tolerance.'],
];

const PRINCIPLES = [
  ['Local-first', 'State and tools live on your machine. A hosted service is an option, never a requirement.'],
  ['No account unless technically necessary', 'Nothing here asks who you are, because nothing here needs to know.'],
  ['Open core', 'The protocol, its specification and its validator are public and MIT-licensed.'],
  ['Model and provider independence', 'Any agent that can read files can continue the work; no vendor owns the memory.'],
  ['Visible state', 'Markdown, JSON and logs a person can open in a text editor, diff and commit.'],
  ['Evidence over claims', 'A check proves it can fail before it is trusted; "done" needs a recorded result.'],
  ['Degrade gracefully where it is safe', 'Missing capabilities lower the mode explicitly instead of failing silently or pretending.'],
  ['Automate the repetition', 'If a human has to repeat the same briefing or click twice a day, it is a tool waiting to be written.'],
  ['The human stays in control', 'Direction, destructive steps and publication are human decisions — but no one should have to babysit every continuation.'],
];

const OPERATOR_TOOLS = PROJECTS.filter((p) => ['ZAICODE', 'AUDAPACK', 'SAIPAL', 'SAICONT', 'LIMISAW'].includes(p.name));
---

<SiteLayout
  title="About"
  tag="/about/"
  description="Who builds SAIPEN and why, the multi-agent workflow behind it, the builder principles, and the engineering contract this website holds itself to."
>
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p><MaturityBadge level="stable" /></p>
  <PixelMark name="saipen-banner-332" width={332} height={120} label="SAIPEN" class="about__banner" />

  <aside class="callout callout--authority about__motto">
    <p class="callout__label">In one line</p>
    <p>
      Built by one human working with replaceable AI agents. The agents change. The project state survives.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <h2 id="author">Who builds SAIPEN</h2>
  <p>
    SAIPEN is created and maintained by <a href={SITE.author} rel="noopener" data-external>vacterro</a> and developed
    through practical multi-agent workflows: AI coding agents from different vendors do much of the implementation,
    and a human operator sets direction, decides, audits and keeps the work continuous across them. The protocol is
    the written-down form of that arrangement.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href={SITE.organization} rel="noopener" data-external>SAIPEN HQ</a> is the organization identity, and the
    <a href={SITE.community} rel="noopener" data-external>SAIPEN Community</a> on Discord is the shared discussion space.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why">Why it exists</h2>
  <p>
    One person running many projects with many agents hits the same wall every day. A session ends, a context limit
    is reached, a provider has a bad hour, a cheaper model takes over for the afternoon — and every new agent arrives
    as a brilliant stranger who has to be briefed from scratch: what are we building, what was already tried, what
    must not be touched, is this actually finished.
  </p>
  <p>
    The person ends up carrying the project between sessions in their own head, retelling it again and again. That is
    the failure SAIPEN removes:
  </p>
  <blockquote><p>The human should not be the project's memory bus.</p></blockquote>
  <p>
    So the memory moved out of the chat and into the project, in plain files, with one exact next step and rules that
    make a cold agent continue instead of asking. Everything else in the protocol — checkpoints, VERIFY, evidence,
    honest WAITs — exists to make that handoff safe.
  </p>

  <h2 id="workflow">How the work is done</h2>
  <ol class="plain flow">
    <li><strong>Idea</strong> — a real problem from running projects, not a hypothetical agent stack.</li>
    <li><strong>Narrow target</strong> — one ticket with a verify contract, small enough to prove.</li>
    <li><strong>Agent</strong> — whichever model and host is available and suited.</li>
    <li><strong>VERIFY</strong> — the strongest available check, with a red control first.</li>
    <li><strong>Audit</strong> — an independent review, often by a different model, packaged as evidence.</li>
    <li><strong>Handoff</strong> — the session ends; the state is already on disk.</li>
    <li><strong>Another agent</strong> — runs <code>saipen continue</code> and picks up the next step.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The operator tooling in the ecosystem grew out of the same loop:
    {
      OPERATOR_TOOLS.map((p, i) => (
        <>
          {i > 0 && (i === OPERATOR_TOOLS.length - 1 ? ' and ' : ', ')}
          <a href={`/ecosystem/#${p.name.toLowerCase()}`}>{p.name}</a>
        </>
      ))
    }. The longer version is in the design note
    <a href="/blog/how-saipen-is-built/">How SAIPEN is built: one operator, many agents</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="principles">Builder principles</h2>
  <p class="w-panel__note">
    These are the author's product instincts across SAIPEN and the tools around it — a fingerprint, not protocol law.
    The normative rules are in the <a href="/spec/">specification</a>.
  </p>
  <dl class="doclist">
    {
      PRINCIPLES.map(([name, line]) => (
        <>
          <dt>{name}</dt>
          <dd>{line}</dd>
        </>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <h2 id="dogfooding">This website is built under SAIPEN</h2>
  <p>
    The site you are reading is itself a SAIPEN project, built by AI agents working through the same lifecycle the
    docs describe. The list below is read at build time from this repository's own <code>.saipen/BOARD.md</code>,
    not typed by hand.
  </p>
  {
    self ? (
      <>
        <dl class="diag">
          <dt>Recorded events</dt>
          <dd>{self.events} in .saipen/LOG.md</dd>
          <dt>Tickets closed</dt>
          <dd>{done.length} of {self.tickets.length}</dd>
          <dt>Last verification</dt>
          <dd>
            site v{LAST_VERIFICATION.siteVersion} on {LAST_VERIFICATION.date}: {LAST_VERIFICATION.pages} pages,{' '}
            {LAST_VERIFICATION.browserChecks} browser checks, {LAST_VERIFICATION.staticGates.length} static gates
          </dd>
        </dl>
        <div class="wide-scroll">
          <table class="w-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Ticket</th>
                <th scope="col">Work</th>
                <th scope="col">State</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {self.tickets.map((t) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{t.id}</th>
                  <td>{t.title}</td>
                  <td>
                    {t.section.toLowerCase()}
                    {t.closure && <span class="w-panel__note"> ({t.closure})</span>}
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Every gate below was shown able to fail before its green result was trusted:</p>
        <ul>
          {LAST_VERIFICATION.redControls.map((c) => (
            <li>{c}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </>
    ) : (
      <p class="w-panel__note">This build was made from a checkout without project memory, so there is nothing to show.</p>
    )
  }

  <h2 id="scope">What this website is</h2>
  <p>
    The public surface of the SAIPEN protocol: documentation, a generated specification reference, the ecosystem
    catalogue and a deterministic playground. It explains and indexes; it does not define.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Not the protocol.</strong> The protocol is the <a href={SITE.repository!} rel="noopener" data-external>SAIPEN repository</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Not an execution engine.</strong> It cannot run an agent and has no backend to run one on.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Authority runs one way.</strong> Canonical protocol source outranks the generated reference, which
      outranks the explanatory docs, which outrank any summary copy. Summary copy never redefines behaviour.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="beyond">Beyond SAIPEN</h2>
  <p>
    The protocol does not exist in a vacuum. The author builds local-first tools for AI agents, automation and
    Windows — AI-agent infrastructure, operator workbenches and quota monitors, desktop utilities, and creative and
    technical software. Only the projects that belong to SAIPEN are catalogued on the
    <a href="/ecosystem/">ecosystem page</a>; the rest are on
    <a href={ECOSYSTEM_SNAPSHOT.author} rel="noopener" data-external>github.com/vacterro</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="design">Why it looks like this</h2>
  <p>
    The site is a native <a href="/ecosystem/#wintage">Wintage</a> interface: Golden Default palette, 2px bevels,
    zero radius, zero shadow, instant interaction — the same design language SAIPEN's own UI.md prescribes for every
    SAIPEN tool. Text is drawn with pixel-grid faces built from openly licensed fonts, so it renders without
    anti-aliasing. The
    <a href="/blog/no-antialiasing-on-the-web/">design note</a> explains how and how it is tested.
  </p>

  <h2 id="accessibility">Accessibility</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A skip link, one <code>main</code>, labelled navigation landmarks and one <code>h1</code> per page, with no skipped heading levels.</li>
    <li>Visible, hard-edged focus on every control; the theme menu is a labelled native select.</li>
    <li>Navigation and disclosures are native elements and work with JavaScript disabled.</li>
    <li>No state is carried by colour alone: every badge and callout has a word.</li>
    <li>Text is real DOM text: selectable, searchable, translatable and readable by screen readers.</li>
    <li>No motion except the one-pixel press of a button; reduced-motion preferences are respected.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="performance">Performance budget</h2>
  <p>
    Mostly static HTML with small scripts. <code>npm run audit:build</code> fails the build when a page exceeds its
    HTML budget, when total JavaScript or font weight passes its ceiling, or when a page loads a script it does not
    need. There is no client framework and no hydration; the twelve pixel faces are 6–17 KB each, and the
    audit fails if any exceeds 20 KB or all of them together exceed 200 KB.
  </p>

  <h2 id="quality-gates">Quality gates</h2>
  <div class="wide-scroll">
    <table class="w-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Command</th><th scope="col">Fails when</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          GATES.map(([cmd, what]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><code>{cmd}</code></th>
              <td>{what}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="deployment">Deployment readiness</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Fully static output with directory-style URLs and a trailing slash on every page.</li>
    <li>Every internal link is root-relative; the canonical origin ({SITE.domain}) is configured in one place.</li>
    <li>A <a href="/404.html">404 page</a>, <a href="/sitemap.xml">sitemap.xml</a>, <a href="/robots.txt">robots.txt</a> and <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a> are generated with the build.</li>
    <li>Aliases such as <code>/spec/latest/</code> are static redirects that need no server rules.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The site is not deployed: attaching a domain and a host is future gate FG-001. Everything it ships is
    redistributable — the pixel faces come from DejaVu Sans and Spleen with their licence notices, checked by
    <code>npm run validate:licenses</code>, and listed with the other shipped assets in THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sources">Sources of this build</h2>
  <dl class="diag">
    <dt>Protocol facts</dt>
    <dd>SAIPEN {meta.version} @ {SHORT_COMMIT}</dd>
    <dt>Ecosystem facts</dt>
    <dd>SAIPEN HQ project map and GitHub, {ECOSYSTEM_SNAPSHOT.date}</dd>
    <dt>Design language</dt>
    <dd>Wintage Golden Default and SAIPEN UI.md</dd>
  </dl>
</SiteLayout>
